## Why This Is Asked
This tests fixed-length path DP and path reconstruction in graphs, a core DP pattern that shows depth beyond standard knapsack.

## Key Concepts
- DP on path length t
- State transition using edges
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time and space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEdgePath(n, edges, k) {
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) dp[v][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const e of edges) {
      const {u,v,w} = e;
      const prev = dp[u][t-1];
      if (prev !== NEG) {
        const cand = prev + w;
        if (cand > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = cand;
          par[v][t] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let bestV = 0, best = NEG;
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) if (dp[v][k] > best) { best = dp[v][k]; bestV = v; }
  const path = [];
  let cur = bestV;
  for (let t=k; t>=1; t--) { path.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; }
  path.push(cur);
  path.reverse();
  return {path, weight: best};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this change if edges could have negative weights? How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over time with multiple constraints: multiple transactions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Two DP states: cash (not holding) and hold (holding).
- Transitions model buy/sell with transaction count t.
- Base cases initialize day 0; backtracking recovers trade days.
- Space optimization possible to O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfit(prices, k){
  const n = prices.length;
  if (n===0 || k===0) return 0;
  const cash = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const hold = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) cash[0][t] = (t===0)?0:-1e15;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
      cash[i][t] = cash[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) cash[i][t] = Math.max(cash[i][t], hold[i-1][t] + prices[i-1]);
      hold[i][t] = hold[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i][t], cash[i-1][t-1] - prices[i-1]);
    }
  }
  let best = 0;
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) best = Math.max(best, cash[n][t]);
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the exact trade days from the DP table?
- How does the solution change if k >= n/2 (unlimited transactions)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a multi-interval DP with per-interval cost and reconstruction. Requires careful state definition and an O(nk) optimization akin to advanced interval DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes and interval counts
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running maximum optimization to avoid O(n^2k)
- Path reconstruction to recover subarray boundaries

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    let best = NEG;
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - S[i-1] + c*(i-1);
      if (val > best) { best = val; }
      const cand = S[i] - c*i + best;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; prv[i][t] = i; }
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct (simple backtrack)
  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prv[i][t] === -1) { i--; continue; }
    const end = i;
    const start = prv[i][t];
    intervals.push([start, end]);
    i = start - 1; t--;
  }
  intervals.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], intervals };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to allow variable per-interval cost or rewards based on length?
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct intervals?

